{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_u3yxuycc7imesio7bwumth2fi2p5srj5tqp5bkwemegbzc24h6aa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done — all four phases pass from a clean slate.\n\n## Phase D gate\n\n| Requirement | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Both programs build, no errors | ✅ `c_src/build/driver`, `translation/target/release/driver` |\n| Every enumerated input: identical stdout/stderr/exit status | ✅ all 257 input classes |\n| `cargo test` passes | ✅ 16/16, debug **and** release |\n| No test disabled/skipped/`#[ignore]`d | ✅ `grep` confirms zero |\n| `c_src/` unmodified | ✅ `main.c` mtime predates my first build; only `c_src/build/` added |\n\n**The input space is small but treacherous.** `main()` reads one byte, so the whole space is `{EOF} ∪ {0x00..=0xFF}` — I tested it **exhaustively** rather than sampling, then swept it a second time with trailing data.\n\nI did the exhaustive sweep as a raw shell diff *first*, before writing any Rust test. The translation already matched on all 256 bytes, which meant I couldn't tell a real suite from a vacuous one. So I mutation-tested the harness: I temporarily made `isalnum` return `1` instead of glibc's bitmask `8`. **9 of 16 tests failed**, then I reverted it. That's the evidence the suite has teeth — worth noting because a green suite against an already-correct translation proves nothing on its own.\n\nSeven traps this program hides, all verified against the compiled C rather than assumed — documented in `translation/ERRORS.md`:\n\n1. **`is*()` print the bitmask, not `1`** — `isalpha('A')` prints `1024`, `isalnum` prints `8`. A \"logically correct\" `0`/`1` port is wrong on every line.\n2. **`char c = getchar()` truncates to negative** — bytes `0x80..=0xFF` index glibc's ctype table *below zero*, which is legal and yields all-zero classifications.\n3. **EOF and byte `0xFF` are indistinguishable** — both truncate to `char -1`, so empty input and `\\xff` produce identical output. Real information loss in the C, preserved not repaired.\n4. **`%c` with `-1`** emits byte `0xFF`.\n5. **Only the first byte is read** — not `scanf` (skips whitespace), not `fgets` (stops at newline).\n6. **Locale is pinned by the program**, so `LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8` must not change output.\n7. **No error path exists at all** — stderr is always empty, status always `0`, even when stdin is a *directory* (`EISDIR`) or stdout is closed.\n\nTrap 7 is where a naive port breaks loudly, and it's the reason I'd flag this file for any future edit: `println!` **panics** on a broken pipe, writing to stderr and exiting `101` where the C silently exits `0`. The translation correctly uses `write_all` on a locked stdout with the `Result` discarded.\n\nOne change to the Rust: `getchar()` now retries on `ErrorKind::Interrupted` instead of treating `EINTR` as EOF, since C's `getchar()` restarts after a signal. I want to be precise that this was **not** an observed mismatch — no test input triggers it; it's a latent fidelity gap I reasoned from the C semantics, and `ERRORS.md` labels it as such rather than dressing it up as a caught bug."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":695,"cache_read_input_tokens":50562,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":695,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e0391790-8749-44a2-8dcf-b581ab137d76","uuid":"885e54e4-d1be-4b3c-9aea-842242720a2e","timestamp":"2026-08-28T14:38:00.017Z"}
